1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,933
Where should we spend computation
and memory to make a sharper image?

2
00:00:06,183 --> 00:00:09,800
We will connect shadow pages,
temporal upscaling and mobile

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:11,333
rendering, in that order.

4
00:00:11,617 --> 00:00:14,567
Engine demonstrations and small
numerical examples will help us follow

5
00:00:14,567 --> 00:00:17,500
the tradeoffs, starting with storing
only the shadows that are needed.

6
00:00:17,533 --> 00:00:20,317
Even when shadows and reflections
look convincing, the final

7
00:00:20,317 --> 00:00:23,100
image does not consist entirely
of freshly computed answers.

8
00:00:23,533 --> 00:00:28,000
This episode connects virtual shadow
maps and modern visibility processing

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,133
to path tracing, ray reconstruction,
and the different roles of DLSS.

10
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:34,483
We finish with a practical framework for

11
00:00:34,483 --> 00:00:36,867
inspecting the resources
of a rendered frame.

12
00:00:37,283 --> 00:00:40,200
First, where should shadow
memory be allocated?

13
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:43,333
We will examine shadow storage
in an Unreal demonstration, then

14
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:46,417
separate rendering from reconstruction
in a Cyberpunk comparison.

15
00:00:46,733 --> 00:00:50,483
Finally, we will follow exposure
controls and a RenderDoc color input.

16
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:53,250
Linking these three
examples gives us a way

17
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:55,767
to ask which stage caused
an image to change.

18
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:01,833
Earlier, a shadow map stored the
closest depth from a light's viewpoint.

19
00:01:02,117 --> 00:01:05,117
A visible surface was
transformed into light space and

20
00:01:05,117 --> 00:01:08,067
compared against that stored
depth to test occlusion.

21
00:01:08,533 --> 00:01:11,400
Virtual shadow maps retain
this fundamental question.

22
00:01:11,783 --> 00:01:14,733
Allocating high resolution across
an entire large region is expensive.

23
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,950
Detailed storage for areas
that are tiny on screen or

24
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:21,133
completely invisible wastes
memory and rendering work.

25
00:01:21,417 --> 00:01:25,300
Meanwhile, a small shadow close to the
camera may need much denser sampling.

26
00:01:25,433 --> 00:01:28,267
A page is a small piece of the larger map.

27
00:01:28,450 --> 00:01:33,100
Its virtual address identifies that piece,
while physical memory holds its contents.

28
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:36,467
Having an address does not
mean every piece is resident.

29
00:01:36,683 --> 00:01:39,483
Our sixteen-piece map and five occupied
slots illustrate this distinction.

30
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:42,267
Unreal Engine's virtual
shadow maps divide a

31
00:01:42,267 --> 00:01:44,867
large virtual address
space into small pages.

32
00:01:45,083 --> 00:01:50,067
Pages needed by the current view receive
physical memory and are rendered.

33
00:01:50,317 --> 00:01:52,117
The size of the virtual
address space must be

34
00:01:52,117 --> 00:01:54,083
distinguished from the
memory actually allocated.

35
00:01:54,383 --> 00:01:56,433
Consider an explanatory grid.

36
00:01:56,533 --> 00:01:59,467
A virtual shadow region
contains sixteen cells, but

37
00:01:59,467 --> 00:02:02,283
surfaces in the current
view reference only five.

38
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:05,967
Allocating memory to those
five illustrates the principle;

39
00:02:05,967 --> 00:02:09,383
these numbers are not measurements
from a particular game.

40
00:02:09,517 --> 00:02:12,683
Look at the colored shadow regions
in the recorded demonstration.

41
00:02:13,167 --> 00:02:16,317
Switching between the scene
and this debug view separates

42
00:02:16,317 --> 00:02:19,067
surface color from a
visualization of shadow data.

43
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:21,667
The colored regions are
not additional lights.

44
00:02:21,933 --> 00:02:24,767
Our sixteen cells were
illustrative; here we

45
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:27,583
observe the regions
displayed by the engine.

46
00:02:27,667 --> 00:02:31,333
In the editor, distinguish the
colored shadow visualization

47
00:02:31,333 --> 00:02:32,767
from the ordinary view.

48
00:02:32,767 --> 00:02:36,717
The colored regions inspect
shadow data; they are not new

49
00:02:36,717 --> 00:02:38,583
colored lights on the wall.

50
00:02:38,750 --> 00:02:42,133
Camera movement reveals different regions
and changes which pages are needed.

51
00:02:42,283 --> 00:02:47,167
If the light and objects remain unchanged,
previously rendered pages can be cached.

52
00:02:47,483 --> 00:02:49,817
Moving a light or
changing a shadow-casting

53
00:02:49,817 --> 00:02:52,267
object requires affected
pages to be updated.

54
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:55,750
Now follow the camera as it moves upward.

55
00:02:55,883 --> 00:02:59,083
Tracking the Ferris wheel
and its shadow shows

56
00:02:59,083 --> 00:03:02,000
the same space from a different viewpoint.

57
00:03:02,167 --> 00:03:06,033
Moving the camera, an object and
a light are different conditions.

58
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:08,850
Recording which one
changed is necessary before

59
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:11,300
comparing newly rendered
data with reused data.

60
00:03:11,333 --> 00:03:16,583
As the camera moves around the Ferris
wheel, identify its structure and

61
00:03:16,583 --> 00:03:18,200
its shadow separately.

62
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:22,567
A changed view can require
different shadow regions.

63
00:03:22,850 --> 00:03:26,783
Apparent softness and which
regions were recomputed

64
00:03:26,783 --> 00:03:28,633
are different questions.

65
00:03:28,917 --> 00:03:33,333
First connect the visible
object with the camera movement.

66
00:03:33,617 --> 00:03:38,883
Keep the wheel as a reference
while separating object movement,

67
00:03:38,883 --> 00:03:43,133
viewpoint movement and
changed lighting conditions.

68
00:03:43,450 --> 00:03:46,450
Similar image changes can
alter the required shadow

69
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:47,800
data in different ways.

70
00:03:47,817 --> 00:03:50,800
Follow surfaces entering the view.

71
00:03:50,933 --> 00:03:57,767
Previous presence does not guarantee
valid reusable shadow data when

72
00:03:57,767 --> 00:04:00,283
objects or lights change.

73
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,667
A page cache divides a large
map so the required regions can

74
00:04:03,667 --> 00:04:04,833
be handled separately.

75
00:04:05,150 --> 00:04:09,050
Establishing actual redraws
also requires the engine’s

76
00:04:09,050 --> 00:04:11,000
invalidation visualization.

77
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:16,833
Also distinguish no longer needed
from no longer valid: skipping an

78
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:21,200
unseen region differs from
discarding stale contents.

79
00:04:21,517 --> 00:04:24,450
The diagram’s address
lookup and update arrows

80
00:04:24,450 --> 00:04:26,433
therefore have different roles.

81
00:04:26,617 --> 00:04:30,217
Soft shadows require more than
a single depth comparison.

82
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:34,883
Multiple samples estimate occlusion and
the penumbra associated with light size.

83
00:04:35,033 --> 00:04:39,217
Unreal's SMRT traces against shadow-map
depth; it must be distinguished

84
00:04:39,217 --> 00:04:42,733
from tracing every ray against
the scene's actual triangles.

85
00:04:42,983 --> 00:04:45,283
Static scenes with
effective caching can cost

86
00:04:45,283 --> 00:04:47,517
differently from
constantly changing scenes.

87
00:04:47,867 --> 00:04:51,367
A useful comparison examines
newly rendered pages, reused

88
00:04:51,367 --> 00:04:54,567
pages, and invalidated
regions alongside resolution.

89
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:57,700
This technique does not solve
global indirect illumination.

90
00:04:58,033 --> 00:05:04,117
In the resource viewer, locate
the shadow physical-page pool.

91
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,950
Virtual addresses identify
regions, while the physical

92
00:05:07,950 --> 00:05:09,400
pool supplies storage.

93
00:05:09,717 --> 00:05:14,017
Connect those roles with the address
and storage cells in the diagram.

94
00:05:14,317 --> 00:05:22,450
A resource name alone does not
reveal this frame’s reuse rate.

95
00:05:22,450 --> 00:05:27,500
Connect the resource view,
the consuming scene and

96
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:29,717
the update conditions.

97
00:05:30,233 --> 00:05:32,100
This investigation method transfers
to another engine even when its

98
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:32,733
implementation differs.

99
00:05:32,917 --> 00:05:35,583
The same memory question
appears in surface textures.

100
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:42,217
A virtual-streaming setting is
followed by the material graph,

101
00:05:42,217 --> 00:05:46,750
connecting the texture
asset with its shader input.

102
00:05:47,133 --> 00:05:52,083
Shadow pages stored light-view depth;
these tiles store surface data.

103
00:05:52,650 --> 00:05:56,350
They share selective residency,
but hold different information and

104
00:05:56,350 --> 00:05:57,700
serve different readers.

105
00:05:58,633 --> 00:06:00,600
Follow inputs sharing
coordinates as nodes are

106
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:01,750
duplicated and reconnected.

107
00:06:01,950 --> 00:06:07,467
The displayed virtual-texture
stack count changes.

108
00:06:07,717 --> 00:06:13,400
Sharing coordinates can allow
address work to be grouped, while

109
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:17,550
different connections
can alter that grouping.

110
00:06:17,767 --> 00:06:21,550
A stack count is not
total frame time or the

111
00:06:21,550 --> 00:06:23,967
number of all texture reads.

112
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:27,500
Read the conversion direction
in the menu:

113
00:06:27,617 --> 00:06:30,667
ordinary textures are being
converted to virtual textures.

114
00:06:30,950 --> 00:06:34,300
Next, the dialog lists affected
textures and materials.

115
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:39,983
The asset and the shader inputs
that read it must agree.

116
00:06:40,917 --> 00:06:45,900
Locate virtual-texture
settings and distinguish tile

117
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:48,017
size from border size.

118
00:06:48,550 --> 00:06:55,850
Larger tiles fetch larger regions;
borders provide neighboring data for

119
00:06:55,850 --> 00:06:59,450
filtering and also consume storage.

120
00:06:59,717 --> 00:07:04,417
Select them with the surface and
filtering requirements in mind.

121
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:10,683
This runtime virtual-texture
asset specifies displacement data.

122
00:07:10,867 --> 00:07:14,267
Runtime-generated surface
results can be cached.

123
00:07:14,467 --> 00:07:17,833
Separate the initial
production cost from later

124
00:07:17,833 --> 00:07:19,617
reads of the stored data.

125
00:07:20,083 --> 00:07:25,683
The next asset stores color
information, alongside tile settings.

126
00:07:25,767 --> 00:07:31,600
Different contents serve different
readers: shadow pages supplied depth

127
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:35,867
comparisons, while this
asset supplies surface data.

128
00:07:36,117 --> 00:07:40,583
Choosing tile size and choosing
content format are separate decisions;

129
00:07:40,583 --> 00:07:44,617
equal dimensions do not imply
equal meaning or production cost.

130
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:50,017
The asset exposes virtual
dimensions and tile settings.

131
00:07:50,333 --> 00:07:55,483
Distinguish the addressable
extent from the resident contents.

132
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:59,533
A large address range does
not mean every texel is

133
00:07:59,533 --> 00:08:01,367
simultaneously resident.

134
00:08:01,517 --> 00:08:05,317
Addressing data also needs
storage, so budget addresses

135
00:08:05,317 --> 00:08:06,983
and contents separately.

136
00:08:07,017 --> 00:08:09,933
These are ordinary
texture-streaming statistics.

137
00:08:10,217 --> 00:08:13,133
Read the Streaming label
and pool-size control before

138
00:08:13,133 --> 00:08:14,517
interpreting the numbers.

139
00:08:14,783 --> 00:08:18,667
Combining them with the virtual-texture
physical pool would hide which

140
00:08:18,667 --> 00:08:19,933
storage is constrained.

141
00:08:20,133 --> 00:08:26,033
Identify the resource
represented by a value first.

142
00:08:26,817 --> 00:08:31,067
Return to virtual-texture pool
settings: default capacity, fixed pools

143
00:08:31,067 --> 00:08:33,733
and editor auto-growth
have different roles.

144
00:08:34,150 --> 00:08:37,500
Discovering capacity while
editing differs from saving a

145
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:39,000
deployment configuration.

146
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:45,083
Record both the resource
type and its pool policy to

147
00:08:45,083 --> 00:08:47,800
reproduce the comparison.

148
00:08:47,950 --> 00:08:54,783
Return to the scene and follow the
camera across floors and walls.

149
00:08:55,133 --> 00:08:59,383
Sharp-looking textures alone
do not reveal the cache state.

150
00:08:59,683 --> 00:09:04,300
Identify newly exposed surfaces during
movement and record the relevant

151
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:07,683
streaming statistics when
a measurement is required.

152
00:09:08,450 --> 00:09:10,750
Another strategy reduces
the cost of evaluating

153
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:12,817
lighting and materials at high resolution.

154
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:17,533
Temporal upscaling renders at a lower
internal resolution, then reconstructs a

155
00:09:17,533 --> 00:09:21,767
higher-resolution image using information
from the current and previous frames.

156
00:09:22,217 --> 00:09:27,783
Unreal's TSR and DLSS Super Resolution
can be compared in this role.

157
00:09:28,117 --> 00:09:30,717
Their implementations and
requirements differ, but both

158
00:09:30,717 --> 00:09:33,483
require separating input
resolution from output resolution.

159
00:09:33,817 --> 00:09:36,550
A sharp image does not
imply that lighting was

160
00:09:36,550 --> 00:09:39,267
evaluated independently
at every output pixel.

161
00:09:39,417 --> 00:09:42,150
Consider only the grid dimensions here.

162
00:09:42,367 --> 00:09:45,800
Eight columns and four rows
give thirty-two pixels.

163
00:09:46,017 --> 00:09:49,617
Halving both dimensions gives
four by two, or eight pixels.

164
00:09:49,850 --> 00:09:52,183
The output can return to
thirty-two pixels even

165
00:09:52,183 --> 00:09:54,517
though the new internal
image began with eight.

166
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:59,050
Halving both width and height reduces
internal pixel count to one quarter.

167
00:09:59,483 --> 00:10:03,733
Frame time does not necessarily
become one quarter, because geometry

168
00:10:03,733 --> 00:10:08,550
processing, shadows, reconstruction
itself, and other fixed costs can remain.

169
00:10:08,750 --> 00:10:12,583
Motion vectors describe
where previous samples moved.

170
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:17,050
Camera projection, jitter conventions,
and object motion must agree.

171
00:10:17,217 --> 00:10:19,417
Incorrect vectors can
retrieve history from a

172
00:10:19,417 --> 00:10:21,667
different surface,
producing ghosting or blur.

173
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,917
Newly exposed regions may
have no valid history.

174
00:10:27,367 --> 00:10:29,633
Transparency, particles,
thin structures, and

175
00:10:29,633 --> 00:10:31,950
interface graphics also
require consideration.

176
00:10:32,217 --> 00:10:35,200
Reconstruction depends on the quality and
validity of the available information.

177
00:10:35,367 --> 00:10:37,700
During this short camera
move, watch windows

178
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:39,950
become visible behind the nearer building.

179
00:10:40,750 --> 00:10:43,017
Those windows were hidden
in the previous image.

180
00:10:43,517 --> 00:10:47,417
Blindly reusing history could bring
back the foreground building instead.

181
00:10:47,900 --> 00:10:50,583
Following motion is
therefore not enough: the

182
00:10:50,583 --> 00:10:53,200
history must also refer
to the same surface.

183
00:10:53,450 --> 00:11:05,717
Follow the moving mannequin’s
silhouette against the background.

184
00:11:06,167 --> 00:11:08,467
Starting with fewer internal pixels
makes valid temporal information

185
00:11:08,467 --> 00:11:10,033
valuable, but a previous
pixel can represent a

186
00:11:10,033 --> 00:11:11,083
different surface after motion.

187
00:11:11,450 --> 00:11:16,383
Reuse therefore requires deciding
which history remains trustworthy.

188
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:21,050
Read internal resolution
separately from output resolution.

189
00:11:21,633 --> 00:11:24,167
Newly revealed background
behind the figure is a

190
00:11:24,167 --> 00:11:25,533
useful observation region.

191
00:11:26,050 --> 00:11:30,183
Continuing to mix the old figure’s
color can blur its boundary; check

192
00:11:30,183 --> 00:11:34,083
whether history and the current
pixel represent the same surface.

193
00:11:34,250 --> 00:11:39,783
Thin rotating blades expose
rapidly changing edges and

194
00:11:39,783 --> 00:11:42,550
newly uncovered background.

195
00:11:43,067 --> 00:11:46,717
Motion vectors help locate corresponding
samples but do not supply the

196
00:11:46,717 --> 00:11:49,233
correct newly revealed
background by themselves.

197
00:11:49,283 --> 00:11:53,950
Compare the mannequin’s broad
silhouette with these thin fast edges:

198
00:11:53,950 --> 00:11:57,250
both use temporal
reconstruction under different

199
00:11:57,250 --> 00:11:59,167
history-validity conditions.

200
00:11:59,533 --> 00:12:02,667
A broad slowly translating surface
and a thin rotating blade present

201
00:12:02,667 --> 00:12:04,333
different correspondence challenges.

202
00:12:04,733 --> 00:12:08,833
Follow the tip and newly revealed
background separately instead of

203
00:12:08,833 --> 00:12:11,183
relying only on an overall impression.

204
00:12:11,217 --> 00:12:14,250
Transfer the question to four games.

205
00:12:14,483 --> 00:12:18,283
In Horizon, read the reconstruction-method
labels and follow the

206
00:12:18,283 --> 00:12:22,483
embroidery on the moving bag, keeping
the same object as the reference.

207
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:26,250
Follow the moving embroidery on
Aloy’s bag and compare its edges under

208
00:12:26,250 --> 00:12:28,167
the two labelled reconstruction methods.

209
00:12:28,583 --> 00:12:30,783
Reconstruction quality and
display frequency are different.

210
00:12:30,950 --> 00:12:34,117
In The Outer Worlds, we inspect
the frame-generation multiplier.

211
00:12:35,467 --> 00:12:39,350
The source changes scenes and
its generation multiplier.

212
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:42,100
Its FPS is the source
demonstration’s value.

213
00:12:42,367 --> 00:12:45,233
Distinguish more frequent
displayed images from more

214
00:12:45,233 --> 00:12:46,767
frequent game-state updates.

215
00:12:46,967 --> 00:12:50,150
The bag comparison reconstructed
detail; this example

216
00:12:50,150 --> 00:12:51,767
examines display frequency.

217
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:54,300
Next inspect light
reconstruction in Cyberpunk.

218
00:12:54,667 --> 00:12:57,650
Read the conditions and identify
reflection and indirect-light

219
00:12:57,650 --> 00:12:59,000
regions in the moving scene.

220
00:12:59,083 --> 00:13:02,550
This DLSS3.5 game demonstration
combines several processing stages.

221
00:13:02,850 --> 00:13:06,583
Follow surface light and
reflections during motion.

222
00:13:06,967 --> 00:13:16,617
It is not an isolated single-feature
fixed-camera experiment.

223
00:13:17,167 --> 00:13:18,533
Compare the role of reconstructing
sparse lighting information with the

224
00:13:18,533 --> 00:13:19,383
different inputs used
for embroidery detail.

225
00:13:19,567 --> 00:13:22,467
In Cyberpunk, distinguish the
reflections on wet pavement

226
00:13:22,467 --> 00:13:23,800
from the signs themselves.

227
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:26,767
The same light appears at
different places on the pavement as

228
00:13:26,767 --> 00:13:27,733
the viewpoint changes.

229
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,050
In the on/off comparison, first
check whether an object disappears or

230
00:13:32,050 --> 00:13:33,583
only its lighting changes.

231
00:13:33,967 --> 00:13:36,883
In the following reconstruction
comparison, follow the

232
00:13:36,883 --> 00:13:38,450
lettering and umbrella edges.

233
00:13:38,650 --> 00:13:42,850
Observe both still-image
clarity and the stability of

234
00:13:42,850 --> 00:13:44,667
detail during movement.

235
00:13:44,867 --> 00:13:49,200
Introduced with DLSS 3 in 2022,
frame generation has a different

236
00:13:49,200 --> 00:13:52,967
role: it inserts display
frames between rendered frames.

237
00:13:53,200 --> 00:13:56,400
An increase in displayed frame
count is not equivalent to the same

238
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:59,600
proportional increase in simulation
or input-processing frequency.

239
00:13:59,900 --> 00:14:02,950
A useful comparison separates
internal resolution,

240
00:14:02,950 --> 00:14:06,317
rendered frame rate, displayed
frame rate, and latency.

241
00:14:06,617 --> 00:14:12,400
A single on-screen number can conflate
responsiveness with visual quality.

242
00:14:13,150 --> 00:14:16,783
Deferred rendering stores depth
for position reconstruction,

243
00:14:16,783 --> 00:14:19,617
normals, and material
attributes in a G-buffer.

244
